WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.520
Well I want to tell you about some numbers.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:03.300 --> 00:00:12.960
We'll start with 2 and go on to 8, pass by 7776&nbsp;
and come finally to 3264. We have a tour to make.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:14.640 --> 00:00:22.800
So how about 2? First of all this is a story about&nbsp;
conics. Conics are curves in the plane that are&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:22.800 --> 00:00:31.860
very familiar to students in high school already.&nbsp;
If I have y equals x squared and I graph that it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:31.860 --> 00:00:36.420
looks something like this, that's a parabola.&nbsp;
Parabolas are important in all sorts of ways&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:36.420 --> 00:00:42.300
but they were also studied, along with the other&nbsp;
so-called conic sections, by the Greeks already&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:42.300 --> 00:00:48.600
for a long time and were a big subject in the 19th&nbsp;
century geometry. So-called conic sections because&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:48.600 --> 00:00:54.720
you can get all of them by slicing a cone. If you&nbsp;
slice it right across straight you get a circle,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:54.720 --> 00:01:01.920
if you tilt your slice a little bit you get an&nbsp;
ellipse, and if you slice it you get a parabola.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:03.060 --> 00:01:09.180
Tilt it a little more, just parallel to the&nbsp;
center, you get part of a hyperbola. If you think&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:09.180 --> 00:01:14.640
of the cone going both ways you get both parts of&nbsp;
the hyperbola. In the 19th century people began to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:14.640 --> 00:01:20.340
think about these in a different way; uh they're&nbsp;
given, all of them, by quadratic equations.&nbsp;

00:01:20.340 --> 00:01:25.860
So here's an equation which is quadratic, has&nbsp;
degree 2, an exponent of 2 and nothing higher.&nbsp;

00:01:25.860 --> 00:01:31.200
And of course a circle can be written as x&nbsp;
squared plus y squared equals 1, for example.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:31.200 --> 00:01:37.020
Ellipse if you put an x coefficient in front of&nbsp;
the x you might get an ellipse. Hyperbola is x&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:37.020 --> 00:01:43.800
times y equals 1. So all of these are quadratic&nbsp;
equations, they have a term of degree 2. All&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:43.800 --> 00:01:49.980
quadratic equations give conics; if you really&nbsp;
include all of them then you get funny things&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:49.980 --> 00:01:54.960
that you might not think of as conics at first.&nbsp;
For example if you just take x squared equals&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:54.960 --> 00:02:01.080
zero - what does that look like? Well here's x&nbsp;
equals zero and x equals zero is the y-axis. X&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:01.080 --> 00:02:06.240
squared equals zero is sort of twice the y-axis,&nbsp;
and that will play an important part in our story.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:07.080 --> 00:02:15.600
Anyway those are conics. They're degree 2 things&nbsp;
for a different reason as well. So one way that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:15.600 --> 00:02:21.480
mathematicians measure the degree of these things&nbsp;
or of other curves is to draw a straight line and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:21.480 --> 00:02:26.040
see how many times it meets them. So let's try&nbsp;
that with a circle: so we agree that a circle&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:26.040 --> 00:02:31.680
is degree 2, it's given by a degree 2 equation, we&nbsp;
drew it. If I draw this straight line it obviously&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:31.680 --> 00:02:38.220
meets it in two points. You might object that well&nbsp;
I cheated a little bit because I didn't draw this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:38.220 --> 00:02:43.500
line which seems to meet it at one point. But&nbsp;
if you think of moving this line towards that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:43.500 --> 00:02:48.540
one then these two points just come together so&nbsp;
I should really count that as two points - and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:48.540 --> 00:02:54.240
then that's two points. But now you'll say, okay&nbsp;
that was fine but how about this line? It doesn't&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:54.240 --> 00:03:00.840
seem to meet it at all. However you may or may not&nbsp;
remember that I did a video about the fundamental&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:00.840 --> 00:03:07.920
theorem of algebra which says that any polynomial&nbsp;
in one variable of degree d has d roots if you&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:07.920 --> 00:03:14.520
count it with multiplicity. That's the case here&nbsp;
but the roots have become imaginary. One way to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:14.520 --> 00:03:18.780
see that, the equation of the circle we drew&nbsp;
before is x squared plus y squared equals 1,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:18.780 --> 00:03:26.040
and if I now substitute y equals ax plus b,
the equation of a line, then I can write this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:26.040 --> 00:03:33.240
as a quadratic equation in x and y. This is&nbsp;
then y squared, would be a squared x squared&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:33.240 --> 00:03:40.680
plus 2 axb plus b squared. If I add an x&nbsp;
squared plus x squared and it's x squared&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:40.680 --> 00:03:46.380
plus one x squared and I've set that equal to&nbsp;
1 - or subtract 1 and look for the roots. Then&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:46.380 --> 00:03:51.540
that will have to have two roots because it's a&nbsp;
polynomial of degree 2. So there are two points,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:51.540 --> 00:03:55.500
they just happen to have imaginary&nbsp;coefficients.
- (Brady: So that line is touching the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:55.500 --> 00:03:58.380
circle) (I just have to imagine it)
- That line touches the circle twice&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:58.380 --> 00:04:05.100
but you imagine it that's right. So that's nice.&nbsp;
Now in the 19th century this was well understood,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:05.100 --> 00:04:09.360
Gauss proved the fundamental theory of&nbsp;
algebra, they knew that this should be true,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:09.360 --> 00:04:15.060
they worked in the complex numbers. But they&nbsp;
went a step further; if you think about this,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:15.060 --> 00:04:19.980
what happens with this vertical line? Where&nbsp;
does it touch the hyperbola? And the answer&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:19.980 --> 00:04:25.020
is it touches it at the point of infinity. If you&nbsp;
continued these lines out they would get closer&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:25.020 --> 00:04:29.280
and closer together; they would never meet but&nbsp;
you can imagine that there's a point out there&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:29.280 --> 00:04:34.860
in infinity. We have a tangency at infinity of&nbsp;
these two lines so that's a double intersection&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:34.860 --> 00:04:40.440
again. And what about this one? Here these two&nbsp;
go out and if we had a line at infinity - as they&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:40.440 --> 00:04:45.420
imagined already in the mid 19th century - this&nbsp;
thing would be tangent to the line of infinity.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:45.420 --> 00:04:50.280
That's what happens when you move that line all&nbsp;
the way up. In fact when you look at equations&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:50.280 --> 00:04:55.440
and allow yourself to make complex changes of&nbsp;
coordinates, all these figures look the same.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:55.440 --> 00:05:01.080
There's no difference between a circle, hyperbola,&nbsp;
parabola in terms of just the equation - they're&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:01.080 --> 00:05:06.780
all degree 2 equations. And as I say they're&nbsp;
degree 2 because they meet a line in two points.

00:05:06.780 --> 00:05:14.160
Now I want to talk about tangencies of&nbsp;
figures, this was a big deal in the 19th&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:14.160 --> 00:05:19.140
century too. So what do I mean by tangency?&nbsp;
Here's the simplest tangency. You can see&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:19.140 --> 00:05:25.560
if I think of a line in the space of lines,&nbsp;
what would that mean? So let's take a point&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:25.560 --> 00:05:29.940
out here and think of all the lines through&nbsp;
the point, so that's what I would mean by a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:29.940 --> 00:05:36.540
line in the space of lines. And you can see&nbsp;
that as I do this there are two places where&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:36.540 --> 00:05:42.660
it's tangent. So that's a condition of degree&nbsp;
2 also. In the space of lines this is a line&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:43.680 --> 00:05:50.400
,this family of lines is a line, and that line&nbsp;
meets the condition of tangency also in two&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:50.400 --> 00:05:56.460
points. So condition of tangency is an equation&nbsp;
of degree 2 on the coordinates of this line. So&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:56.460 --> 00:06:00.720
that's another way that you can have degree&nbsp;
2. So now let's talk about the first number,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:00.720 --> 00:06:06.480
and another 2. Suppose I look at tangency of&nbsp;
two circles. So two circles can be tangent, for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:06.480 --> 00:06:09.720
example this way - here are two&nbsp;tangent circles.
- (They're just kissing each other)&nbsp;

00:06:09.720 --> 00:06:15.660
Kissing each other, they could kiss on the inside&nbsp;
too like this. And a line in the space of circles,&nbsp;

00:06:15.660 --> 00:06:20.580
what would that mean? Well here's- here's how I&nbsp;
draw a circle but I could have other radii right?&nbsp;

00:06:20.580 --> 00:06:24.420
I could put an r in there, that would be&nbsp;
the radius of the circle. Turns out if I&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:24.420 --> 00:06:29.040
make r bigger and smaller that's a line in&nbsp;
the space of circles. Here is one circle,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:29.040 --> 00:06:34.440
I'm going to fix this circle and I'm going to&nbsp;
take another circle and change its radius. So&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:34.440 --> 00:06:39.540
I'm going to fix the center, the center here is&nbsp;
(0,0), and I'm going to change the radius and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:39.540 --> 00:06:45.660
watch it and it expands and at a certain point it&nbsp;
becomes tangent to this circle and then it stops&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:45.660 --> 00:06:51.060
being tangent afterwards. After a while it'll&nbsp;
be a circle which has this as its center which&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:51.060 --> 00:06:57.300
looks like this and is tangent up here. It has a&nbsp;
second tangency right? So in the space of circles,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:57.300 --> 00:07:03.180
being tangent is a degree 2 condition.
Right? That happens twice.&nbsp;

00:07:03.180 --> 00:07:06.420
- (And it can't happen a third&nbsp;time?)
- That can't happen a third time,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:06.420 --> 00:07:10.200
even over the complex numbers, you have to&nbsp;
take my word for that. But we could work it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:10.200 --> 00:07:14.340
out in equations and then it would be easy.&nbsp;
Here's another way the degree plays a role:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:14.340 --> 00:07:18.960
we saw that a line meets something of degree 2&nbsp;
in two points - how about two things of degree&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:18.960 --> 00:07:25.620
2? An ellipse and a circle both have a degree&nbsp;
2 and they can meet in four points, 2 times 2.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:26.640 --> 00:07:30.480
And it's something called Bézout's&nbsp;
Theorem, a very important theorem in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:30.480 --> 00:07:33.780
geometry, Étienne Bézout I think.
- (But didn't your two circles&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:33.780 --> 00:07:36.360
both) (have degree 2?)
- Yeah these both have degree&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:36.360 --> 00:07:41.160
2 but they- and they look like they're&nbsp;
meeting only in two points at a time&nbsp;

00:07:41.160 --> 00:07:43.740
- (Oh they're meeting, sorry,&nbsp;
that's meeting not tangenting yeah)

00:07:43.740 --> 00:07:49.200
That's right.&nbsp;But even so these circles look like&nbsp;
they're meeting only in two points right but this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:49.200 --> 00:07:55.560
is a case like this- these lines, if we do&nbsp;
it in equations then we could parameterize&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:55.560 --> 00:08:00.060
the circle and substitute the equation of the&nbsp;
other circle in it then we'd get an equation&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:00.060 --> 00:08:07.140
of degree 4 and it would have 4 roots. So just as&nbsp;
we computed here the degree 2 we'd have 4 roots.&nbsp;

00:08:07.140 --> 00:08:12.780
So over the complex numbers they always have&nbsp;
four intersections counted with multiplicity. Two&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:12.780 --> 00:08:19.260
conics. And that's a case of Bézout's theorem:&nbsp;
if you have a bunch of conditions of degree d&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:19.920 --> 00:08:26.760
where you expect d solutions from a line, but&nbsp;
if you have two of them then they meet in d&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:26.760 --> 00:08:32.520
squared points. If you have three of them&nbsp;
meeting in a 3-dimensional space like three&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:32.520 --> 00:08:38.160
spheres would meet in eight points over the&nbsp;
complex numbers. So that's a general fact. So&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:38.160 --> 00:08:43.680
how many circles are there? What is the space&nbsp;
of circles like? So if you think of circles,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:43.680 --> 00:08:49.020
there are really three numbers that are important:&nbsp;
there's a center, let's say the point (a,b),&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:49.020 --> 00:08:56.160
and there's the radius, the equation x minus a&nbsp;
squared plus x minus b squared equals r squared.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:56.160 --> 00:09:01.080
So that's the equation of a general circle&nbsp;
and there are three numbers involved. So the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:01.080 --> 00:09:05.820
space of circles is a 3-dimensional space.&nbsp;
Now if you have a 3-dimensional space and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:05.820 --> 00:09:13.680
you have conditions which are satisfied by some-&nbsp;
somewhere on a line, let's say two in a line, those&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:13.680 --> 00:09:19.860
are quadratic conditions, then Bézout's theorem&nbsp;
tells you if you have three such conditions there&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:19.860 --> 00:09:26.880
ought to be three points of intersection. So Bézout&nbsp;
interpreted that funny way says there should be&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:26.880 --> 00:09:35.400
2 times 2 times 2 - 8 circles - that fulfill all of
three degree 2 conditions, like being tangent to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:35.400 --> 00:09:42.360
a given circle. So we take three circles - so here's&nbsp;
one, and here's one, and here's one. If you worked&nbsp;&nbsp;

