they're round. But conics, as I said they come&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:04.440 --> 00:16:09.300
in lots of shapes and if you let the equations&nbsp;
degenerate a little bit; if you take b equals c&nbsp;

00:16:09.300 --> 00:16:16.680
equals d equals e equals zero you get x squared&nbsp;
equals zero, and that's the equation of a double line.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:17.400 --> 00:16:23.280
So the double lines could be in there; and&nbsp;
if you think about it what is the condition&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:23.280 --> 00:16:29.160
of tangency? It's that it where it touches it&nbsp;
touches twice. But a double line touches twice&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:29.160 --> 00:16:34.800
wherever it touches right? There's there's two&nbsp;
lines there so it's it's tangent. And how many&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:34.800 --> 00:16:40.440
double lines are there? Not just finitely many but&nbsp;
a whole plane full. Because- you know you can draw&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:40.440 --> 00:16:45.900
one parameter family of lines through a point,&nbsp;
you can move the point so you have a two parameter&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:45.900 --> 00:16:52.140
family of lines so there's a whole surface&nbsp;
of lines. So when you intersected those five&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:52.740 --> 00:16:59.460
conditions of degree 6 you got not finitely&nbsp;
many solutions but a surface of double lines,&nbsp;

00:17:00.180 --> 00:17:07.440
plus the solutions you really wanted. And Chasles had&nbsp;
to figure out how to remove the surface and write&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:07.440 --> 00:17:14.280
down the conditions without the surface. He got&nbsp;
the right answer by methods which are not rigorous&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:14.280 --> 00:17:21.840
by our standards; and probably the first rigorous&nbsp;
demonstration of this number is due to Fulton and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:21.840 --> 00:17:27.900
MacPherson in the 1970s. So it took a long time to&nbsp;get this right. 
